<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="dishwasher-repair/kitchenaid-not-draining"
  eyebrow="KitchenAid Dishwasher Not Draining · F9E1 Code · Same Day Southern California"
  title="KitchenAid Dishwasher Not Draining: Causes and Fix"
  subtitle="Standing water in a KitchenAid, or an F9E1 code, is usually the filter, drain hose, disposal plug, or pump. Here's the order to check and when to call."
  heroImageTitle="KitchenAid Dishwasher Not Draining"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">KitchenAid won't drain? Check the filter you forgot you had.</h2>

    <p><strong>Standing water in a KitchenAid dishwasher — and the F9E1 code, which just means the pump-out didn't finish in time — almost always traces to a clogged lower filter, the drain hose (a kink, a clog, or a loop that sits too low), the disposal knockout plug after a new disposal, a blocked sink air gap, or a failed drain pump. The first four are safe to check yourself; the pump is where our techs take over. Because KitchenAid tubs run so quietly, most owners never clean the filter, so that's step one. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>KitchenAid dishwashers drain through the kitchen sink and, in most homes, the garbage disposal, so many "KitchenAid won't drain" calls our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair take turn out to be a packed filter or a sink-side blockage, not a broken machine. Right order, fewer surprises.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 SHARED PLATFORM -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Premium badge, Whirlpool platform</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Same machine as Whirlpool and Maytag underneath.</h2>

    <p><strong>KitchenAid, Maytag, and many Kenmore (665-series) dishwashers are built on the same Whirlpool platform.</strong> They share the F9E1 drain code, the same twist-out filter, the same drain pump and check valve, and the same drain path to the sink. KitchenAid sits at the premium end — quieter tubs, third racks, ProWash cycles — but the drain diagnosis is identical to a Whirlpool. That's good news: the fix is usually a five-minute filter clean, not an expensive premium part. Same platform, brand pages: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/whirlpool-not-draining/">Whirlpool not draining</a>, <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/maytag-not-draining/">Maytag not draining</a>, <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/kenmore-not-draining/">Kenmore not draining</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · The causes, in order</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Five causes, most common first.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>1. Clogged lower filter</h3>
      <p>The cylindrical filter under the lower spray arm packs with food, glass grit, and grease — especially on quiet KitchenAid tubs owners forget to service. A partial clog slows the drain until F9E1 trips; a full one stops it. Twist it out, rinse, clear the sump.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>2. Drain hose — kink, clog, or low loop</h3>
      <p>Check behind the unit for a crushed or kinked hose, and confirm it loops up high before dropping to the drain. A low loop lets sink water siphon back and pool.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>3. Disposal knockout plug (new install)</h3>
      <p>A new garbage disposal ships with a solid plug sealing the dishwasher drain port. If it wasn't punched out, the dishwasher can't drain. This is the answer when a KitchenAid stops draining right after disposal work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>4. Blocked air gap (common in California)</h3>
      <p>Many California kitchens use an air gap — the small chrome cap on the counter — to meet the plumbing code's backflow rule. It clogs and backs water into the dishwasher. Popping it open and clearing it is a two-minute fix.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>5. Failed drain pump or check valve</h3>
      <p>When the whole path is clear and water still stands (or F9E1 returns), the drain pump has usually failed or its check valve is stuck open. This one needs a meter and a replacement part.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE CHECKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Five safe steps before you call.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Scoop the standing water</strong> with a cup and sponge so you can reach the filter.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean the filter.</strong> Twist out the cylindrical filter under the lower spray arm, rinse it, and clear the sump — watch for broken glass.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the drain hose and high loop.</strong> Straighten any kink, and route the hose so it loops up high before dropping to the drain.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the garbage disposal</strong> with water, and if it's a new disposal, confirm the knockout plug was removed.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the air gap.</strong> Pop the chrome cap and clean it out. Use only dishwasher detergent, never dish soap.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> filter, hose, disposal, air gap, and a 30-second power-cycle are all safe. Don't remove the drain pump or open the control yourself, and mind the glass in the sump. If water still stands after these steps, it's the pump or the control — our job.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 WHEN TO CALL -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clean path, still standing water? It's the pump.</h2>

    <p>Once the filter, hose, disposal, and air gap are verified clear and the tub still holds water (or F9E1 keeps returning), the drain pump or its check valve is the likely fault, and that needs testing and a replacement part. Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair carry the common Whirlpool-platform drain pumps, so a confirmed failure is usually a one-visit fix. We check the upstream path first so we never swap a good pump that was fighting a clog, then quote before any work. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong> Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dishwasher-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, Orange, and Ventura in most cases.</p>

    <p>Related: the general <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-draining/">dishwasher not draining</a> page, the <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/error-codes/">dishwasher error codes</a> guide, and brand coverage on <a href="/brands/kitchenaid-dishwasher-repair/">KitchenAid dishwasher repair</a>. Same platform: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/whirlpool-not-draining/">Whirlpool</a>, <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/maytag-not-draining/">Maytag</a>, <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/kenmore-not-draining/">Kenmore</a>. Full coverage on the <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/">dishwasher repair</a> hub. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">KitchenAid dishwasher not draining: common questions.</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (<details class="faq-item"><summary>{f.q}</summary><p set:html={f.a}></p></details>))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>KitchenAid dishwasher still full of water? Call today.</h2>
